		"Integrate custom OpenGL rendering within an SFML window": {
			"name": "Integrate custom OpenGL rendering within an SFML window",
			"simSteps": [
				{
					"simStepId": "3cec0edd-bc77-44fb-99c4-443239d1c099",
					"diagramNodeId": "6dc8c74f-4432-4f74-a5be-d3cff0f0b185",
					"simStepLabel": "1. Create SFML Window with Custom OpenGL Context",
					"simStepDescription": "The application requests a 24-bit depth buffer by creating a `sf::ContextSettings` object. It then creates an `sf::RenderWindow`, passing these settings. This tells SFML to create a window with an underlying OpenGL context that has a depth buffer, which is essential for 3D rendering.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "48",
						"endLine": "56",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"sf::ContextSettings",
							"contextSettings",
							"sf::RenderWindow",
							"window"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"depthBits\": 24, \"stencilBits\": 0, \"antiAliasingLevel\": 0, \"majorVersion\": 1, \"minorVersion\": 1, \"attributeFlags\": \"Default\", \"sRgbCapable\": false}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"window_handle\": \"0x1a2b3c4d\", \"context_id\": \"0x5e6f7g8h\", \"status\": \"created\"}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "465967be-83b0-4d81-9033-4cb57dd7dbf1",
					"diagramNodeId": "338d44ea-6e31-44c6-903c-111bf8d332f5",
					"simStepLabel": "2. Window Ready for OpenGL",
					"simStepDescription": "The newly created `sf::RenderWindow` object, with its associated OpenGL context, is now available to the application. The application can proceed to activate it for rendering.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "src/SFML/Graphics/RenderWindow.cpp",
						"startLine": "85",
						"endLine": "87",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"Window::setActive(active)",
							"RenderTarget::setActive(active)"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"window_handle\": \"0x1a2b3c4d\", \"context_id\": \"0x5e6f7g8h\", \"status\": \"created\"}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"window_handle\": \"0x1a2b3c4d\", \"context_id\": \"0x5e6f7g8h\", \"status\": \"created\"}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "ccbe2db4-f112-423a-8715-aa1250077b38",
					"diagramNodeId": "89013c90-0bd2-4e42-9f56-53849d9760ca",
					"simStepLabel": "3. Activate Window's OpenGL Context",
					"simStepDescription": "The application calls `window.setActive(true)` to make the window's OpenGL context the current one for the calling thread. This is a mandatory step before any raw OpenGL commands can be issued.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "88",
						"endLine": "92",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"window.setActive"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"active\": true}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"success\": true}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "59356bed-3be1-41e5-83a0-0b4bd735be0b",
					"diagramNodeId": "9872c19b-eee3-42b8-979e-91e08be577f2",
					"simStepLabel": "4. Context Activation Call Chain",
					"simStepDescription": "The `RenderWindow::setActive` call is propagated to `Window::setActive`, which in turn calls the underlying `GlContext::setActive`. This function handles the platform-specific details of making the OpenGL context current.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "src/SFML/Window/Window.cpp",
						"startLine": "178",
						"endLine": "181",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"m_context->setActive(active)"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"active\": true}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"active\": true}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "d112f9d2-8b80-4172-a4a1-60bdfdde5ae0",
					"diagramNodeId": "5ec50018-bf31-4d23-8394-45c6bfd2c8ab",
					"simStepLabel": "5. Initialize OpenGL States",
					"simStepDescription": "With the context active, the application can now issue raw OpenGL commands. It enables depth testing (`glEnable(GL_DEPTH_TEST)`) and sets the clear depth value (`glClearDepth`). These are common setup steps for 3D rendering.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "100",
						"endLine": "106",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"glEnable",
							"GL_DEPTH_TEST",
							"glDepthMask",
							"glClearDepth"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "null",
					"outputDataExample": "null"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "d931cb8c-58b6-490c-a5fe-a50334a95a28",
					"diagramNodeId": "644f3321-7f9d-46ec-95ea-81bf44a7e989",
					"simStepLabel": "6. OpenGL Commands Sent to GPU",
					"simStepDescription": "The OpenGL state change commands are sent through the graphics driver to the GPU, configuring the rendering pipeline for subsequent draw calls.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "extlibs/headers/glad/include/glad/gl.h",
						"startLine": "20371",
						"endLine": "20371",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"sf_glad_glEnable"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"commands\": [\"glEnable(GL_DEPTH_TEST)\", \"glClearDepth(1.f)\"]}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"commands\": [\"glEnable(GL_DEPTH_TEST)\", \"glClearDepth(1.f)\"]}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "34707409-117b-4897-9501-e1db9fd26727",
					"diagramNodeId": "988a3d1e-3ec1-46e0-a623-29836012b109",
					"simStepLabel": "7. Clear Buffers for New Frame",
					"simStepDescription": "Inside the main rendering loop, the application clears the depth buffer using `glClear(GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT)`. This ensures that depth information from the previous frame does not interfere with the current frame's rendering.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "295",
						"endLine": "296",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"glClear",
							"GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"clear_mask\": \"GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT\"}",
					"outputDataExample": "null"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "63648adb-9dbf-481b-90b4-5d9b13584cef",
					"diagramNodeId": "24200058-bd8a-487f-9524-14dfe33a52fd",
					"simStepLabel": "8. GPU Buffer Clear Command",
					"simStepDescription": "The `glClear` command is sent to the GPU, instructing it to erase the contents of the specified buffers (in this case, the depth buffer) and fill them with the pre-defined clear values.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "extlibs/headers/glad/include/glad/gl.h",
						"startLine": "10620",
						"endLine": "10621",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"glClear",
							"sf_glad_glClear"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"command\": \"glClear\", \"params\": {\"mask\": \"GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT\"}}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"command\": \"glClear\", \"params\": {\"mask\": \"GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT\"}}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "1d15d922-6788-4295-871d-41e7524c045e",
					"diagramNodeId": "c1a6a40c-e8dd-44e7-ae65-d8f8f41b8ffa",
					"simStepLabel": "9. Render 3D Object with OpenGL",
					"simStepDescription": "The application sets up transformations for the 3D object using OpenGL's matrix stack (`glTranslatef`, `glRotatef`) and then issues a `glDrawArrays` call to render the cube's vertices. This demonstrates drawing custom 3D geometry.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "311",
						"endLine": "319",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"glTranslatef",
							"glRotatef",
							"glDrawArrays"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"vertices_count\": 36, \"transformations\": {\"translate\": [0,0,-200], \"rotate_x_angle\": 120.5, \"rotate_y_angle\": 72.3, \"rotate_z_angle\": 216.9}}",
					"outputDataExample": "null"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "f34f5eb4-ebcd-424a-ab67-8e09abcc9461",
					"diagramNodeId": "d4f647cb-32ca-4f3a-b928-423187bbb036",
					"simStepLabel": "10. Vertex Data Sent to GPU Pipeline",
					"simStepDescription": "The vertex data for the cube, previously loaded into a vertex buffer, is sent to the GPU's rendering pipeline to be processed by the vertex and fragment shaders, ultimately resulting in pixels being written to the framebuffer.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "319",
						"endLine": "319",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"glDrawArrays"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"command\": \"glDrawArrays\", \"params\": {\"mode\": \"GL_TRIANGLES\", \"first\": 0, \"count\": 36}}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"command\": \"glDrawArrays\", \"params\": {\"mode\": \"GL_TRIANGLES\", \"first\": 0, \"count\": 36}}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "d1cec5d0-28bd-4655-848d-ae13b1c261e9",
					"diagramNodeId": "82370d76-77d1-4e9a-902b-e36306119031",
					"simStepLabel": "11. Deactivate OpenGL Context",
					"simStepDescription": "After finishing direct OpenGL calls, the application deactivates the context by calling `window.setActive(false)`. This is good practice for interoperability, allowing SFML's 2D drawing functions to work correctly without conflicting states.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "examples/opengl/OpenGL.cpp",
						"startLine": "322",
						"endLine": "326",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"window.setActive"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"active\": false}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"success\": true}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "c621163e-763d-48f5-8203-66f6706ee50f",
					"diagramNodeId": "413ca6c7-38ea-4312-82a3-f8f9b1e2c04e",
					"simStepLabel": "12. Framebuffer Ready for Display",
					"simStepDescription": "With all rendering for the frame complete, the contents of the window's back buffer, which now contains both SFML-drawn and OpenGL-drawn elements, are ready to be displayed.",
					"isEdge": 1,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "src/SFML/Window/Window.cpp",
						"startLine": "198",
						"endLine": "201",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"setActive",
							"m_context->display"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "{\"frame_status\": \"rendered\", \"buffer\": \"back\"}",
					"outputDataExample": "{\"frame_status\": \"rendered\", \"buffer\": \"back\"}"
				},
				{
					"simStepId": "7b48a43e-8489-499b-b636-961b5776080d",
					"diagramNodeId": "e4ede59c-9139-41d9-9394-cc04c89bce7e",
					"simStepLabel": "13. Display the Final Frame",
					"simStepDescription": "The application calls `window.display()` to swap the back buffer (where rendering occurred) with the front buffer, making the composed scene visible on the screen. The `display` method internally handles activating the context if it is not already active.",
					"isEdge": 0,
					"sourceCodeMapping": {
						"filePath": "src/SFML/Window/Window.cpp",
						"startLine": "198",
						"endLine": "201",
						"relevantVariables": [
							"display",
							"setActive",
							"m_context->display"
						]
					},
					"inputDataExample": "null",
					"outputDataExample": "null"
				}
			],
			"description": "<ul><li>For developers needing more advanced or 3D rendering capabilities, SFML provides seamless interoperability with OpenGL</li><li>- SFML creates and manages an OpenGL context for any window it opens</li><li>- A developer can activate the window's context (<code>window</li><li>setActive(true)</code>) and then issue raw OpenGL commands</li><li>- It's possible to mix SFML's easy-to-use 2D drawing functions with custom OpenGL rendering in the same application, for example, to draw a 3D scene with a 2D interface on top</li></ul>",
			"simulationNodesAndEdges": {
				"6dc8c74f-4432-4f74-a5be-d3cff0f0b185": {
